From: AR0234 port <local>
Subject: [PATCH 0008] vin: make the second VI channel work, and stop silent
 pipeline corruption

All of these were found while bringing up the vendor's "one sensor, two video
nodes" topology (/dev/video0 = vinc0/vipp0, /dev/video4 = vinc4/vipp1, both on
the same mipi0/csi0/tdm_rx0/isp0) on the Orange Pi Zero 3W, and while preparing
the second physical camera.

1) vin_core.c - vinc%d_isp_tx_ch != 0 silently kills the video node

   isp_tx_ch is what csic_vipp_input_select() puts into CSIC_VIPPx_IN, i.e. it
   names the ISP output channel feeding this VIPP. The ISP602 here has a single
   output channel, so any other value points the VIPP at a source that is never
   driven: scaler and DMA are programmed correctly, nothing is logged and the
   node just never delivers a frame.

   Measured (2026-09-16, module 0007, /dev/video4 with vinc4_isp_tx_ch = <1>):
     CSIC_VIPP1_IN (0x58008a4) = 1 -> 0 frames in 12 s, dmesg completely silent
     same run, 0x58008a4 written back to 0 by hand -> 119.07 fps median
     (8.398 ms, 0 outliers), and it kept running next to /dev/video0.
   So force the only channel this SoC has, and say so at ERR level.

2) vin_video.c - a second node's S_FMT rewrote the *running* uplink

   vin_pipeline_try_format() walks the media graph from the capture subdev with
   which = ACTIVE and calls set_fmt() on sensor/mipi/csi/tdm_rx/isp. With two
   vincs on one CSI (or just a stale S_FMT from the other node) that changes the
   format the first stream is running with. Measured:
     * sensor switched to 960x600 while scaler.0 still expected 1920x1080
       -> vi0 lost_cnt 647 of 648 frames, capture process stuck until killed;
     * the earlier IOMMU fault of the same family:
       "Bug is in CSI module, invalid address: 0x0, id:0x2"  (master 2 = ISP).
   Now: S_FMT/STREAMON refuse loudly (-EBUSY, with a message naming both nodes)
   instead of corrupting a running stream. The pre-flight check runs before
   vin_auto_s_input() powers anything up, so a rejected S_FMT leaves no
   half-open video node behind (that state alone degraded the other camera to
   18 fps - reproduced with the 3A service stopped as well, so it is not 3A).

3) vin.c - a capture node with no sensor was dropped silently

   Enabling vinc20 (device_id 8, the vendor's second camera path at
   mipi1/csi1/isp0) without a sensor in slot 2 produced no /dev/video8 and not a
   single log line. Now it says which vinc, which sensor slot and why.

4) sunxi_isp.c - refuse the virtual ISP instances

   isp@58ffffc & friends are aliases of the same ISP602 (the reg windows of
   isp00/isp01/isp02/isp03 all end at 0x5901300) and the driver used to register
   them as empty media entities while the logic ISP works in online mode. Keep
   the node disabled: with `status = "okay"` the *first* camera stops delivering
   frames with no error at all (measured twice). The probe now fails with an
   explicit message instead of registering a dead entity.

   NB: this is a loud failure, not a cure - the breakage is caused by the
   device tree node being enabled, not by the subdev being registered: with the
   probe refused (no sunxi_isp.1, no extra media entity, identical media graph)
   /dev/video0 still streamed 0 frames in 30 s.

5) sunxi_scaler.c / vin_video.c - G_SELECTION noise

   sunxi_scaler_subdev_get_selection() answered -EINVAL for every target except
   CROP/CROP_BOUNDS, and vin_video.c printed that at ERR level, so every
   v4l2-ctl --all / libisp start left "v4l2 sub device scaler get_selection
   error!" in dmesg on a healthy board. Report the sink rectangle instead.
   Same for the three messages that only mean "the node was used after its
   sensor went away" ("is not used, videoN cannot be close", "has already
   stream off"): downgraded to warnings.
--- a/bsp/drivers/vin/vin.c
+++ b/bsp/drivers/vin/vin.c
@@ -1883,6 +1883,19 @@
 		module = &vind->modules[vind->vinc[i]->rear_sensor];
 
 		if (module->sensors.valid_idx == NO_VALID_SENSOR) {
+			/*
+			 * This capture node is dropped because the sensor slot it
+			 * points at has no sensor bound - the sensor driver did
+			 * not probe (not wired, not powered, or not there at
+			 * all).  The vendor code only does vind->vinc[i] = NULL
+			 * here, so the failure is invisible: no /dev/videoN, no
+			 * message, nothing at all in dmesg.  Say it out loud.
+			 */
+			vin_err("vinc%d (device_id %d) needs sensor%d (vinc%d_rear_sensor_sel = %d) but no sensor is bound to that slot, so no /dev/video%d will be created\n",
+				vind->vinc[i]->id, vind->vinc[i]->id,
+				vind->vinc[i]->rear_sensor,
+				vind->vinc[i]->id, vind->vinc[i]->rear_sensor,
+				vind->vinc[i]->id);
 			vind->vinc[i] = NULL;
 			continue;
 		}
--- a/bsp/drivers/vin/vin-video/vin_core.c
+++ b/bsp/drivers/vin/vin-video/vin_core.c
@@ -2164,6 +2164,30 @@
 	if (of_property_read_u32(np, property_name, &vinc->isp_tx_ch))
 		vinc->isp_tx_ch = 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * isp_tx_ch ends up in csic_vipp_input_select() as
+	 *     vipp_input[vipp_virtual_find_sel[vipp_sel]][isp_virtual_find_sel[isp_sel]][isp_tx_ch]
+	 * (top_reg.c), i.e. it names the ISP output channel that feeds this
+	 * VIPP; on sun60iw2 that table is the identity one (isp * 4 + ch).
+	 * The ISP602 of this SoC has a single output channel, so any value != 0
+	 * points the VIPP at a source that is never driven: the scaler and its
+	 * DMA are programmed correctly, nothing is logged, and the video node
+	 * simply never receives a frame.
+	 *
+	 * Measured on the board 2026-09-16 (/dev/video4 = vinc4, vipp1):
+	 *   vinc4_isp_tx_ch = <1> -> CSIC_VIPP1_IN (0x58008a4) = 1
+	 *                            0 frames in 12 s, dmesg completely silent
+	 *   same run, 0x58008a4 written back to 0 by hand
+	 *                            119.07 fps median (8.398 ms), 0 outliers,
+	 *                            and it kept streaming next to /dev/video0.
+	 * Keep only channels the ISP really has, and say so.
+	 */
+	if (vinc->isp_tx_ch != 0) {
+		vin_err("vinc%d_isp_tx_ch = %u, but isp%d has a single output channel on this SoC; forcing 0 (video%d would otherwise silently never receive a frame)\n",
+			pdev->id, vinc->isp_tx_ch, vinc->isp_sel, pdev->id);
+		vinc->isp_tx_ch = 0;
+	}
+
 	sprintf(property_name, "vinc%d_tdm_rx_sel", pdev->id);
 	if (of_property_read_u32(np, property_name, &vinc->tdm_rx_sel))
 		vinc->tdm_rx_sel = 0;
--- a/bsp/drivers/vin/vin-video/vin_video.c
+++ b/bsp/drivers/vin/vin-video/vin_video.c
@@ -827,6 +827,70 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Is this subdev currently driven by *another* capture node that is
+ * streaming?  The sensor / mipi / csi / tdm_rx / isp path is shared between
+ * all the vincs that hang off one CSI, so whoever calls set_fmt on it with
+ * which = ACTIVE is also changing the format the *running* stream sees.
+ * (Kernel 6.1 removed media_entity.stream_count, and the vendor never calls
+ * media_pipeline_start(), so ask the capture nodes themselves.)
+ */
+static bool vin_sd_used_by_other_stream(struct vin_core *vinc,
+					struct v4l2_subdev *sd, int sd_ind)
+{
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < VIN_MAX_DEV; i++) {
+		struct vin_core *vd = vin_core_gbl[i];
+
+		if (vd == NULL || vd == vinc)
+			continue;
+		if (!vin_streaming(&vd->vid_cap))
+			continue;
+		if (vd->vid_cap.pipe.sd[sd_ind] == sd)
+			return true;
+	}
+	return false;
+}
+
+/*
+ * S_FMT on a second capture node must not resize the sensor/mipi/csi/isp path
+ * that another, already streaming node is using.  Doing so is what produced
+ *   "Bug is in CSI module, invalid address: 0x0, id:0x2"  (IOMMU master 2 = ISP)
+ * and, more often, a permanent "input lines ... frame lost!" state where one
+ * scaler expects 1920x1080 while the parser only delivers 960x600.
+ *
+ * This is the cheap, side-effect-free version of the check (it compares the
+ * other capture node's idea of the sensor window), used before anything is
+ * powered up; vin_pipeline_try_format() repeats it on the real subdevs.
+ */
+static int vin_check_shared_uplink(struct vin_core *vinc, unsigned int w,
+				   unsigned int h)
+{
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < VIN_MAX_DEV; i++) {
+		struct vin_core *vd = vin_core_gbl[i];
+
+		if (vd == NULL || vd == vinc)
+			continue;
+		if (!vin_streaming(&vd->vid_cap))
+			continue;
+		if (vd->csi_sel != vinc->csi_sel || vd->mipi_sel != vinc->mipi_sel)
+			continue;
+		if (!vd->vin_status.width || !vd->vin_status.height)
+			continue;
+		if (vd->vin_status.width == w && vd->vin_status.height == h)
+			continue;
+
+		vin_err("video%d is streaming %ux%u from the same sensor, refusing the %ux%u S_FMT on video%d (the uplink cannot serve two sizes at once)\n",
+			vd->id, vd->vin_status.width, vd->vin_status.height,
+			w, h, vinc->id);
+		return -EBUSY;
+	}
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static int vin_pipeline_try_format(struct vin_core *vinc,
 				    struct v4l2_mbus_framefmt *tfmt,
 				    struct vin_fmt **fmt_id,
@@ -936,6 +1000,52 @@
 					sfmt.reserved[0] = ch_id;
 			}
 
+			/*
+			 * The sensor/mipi/csi/tdm_rx/isp path is shared by every
+			 * vinc that hangs off the same CSI, and this walk runs
+			 * with which = ACTIVE, so a second video node's S_FMT
+			 * silently rewrites the *running* uplink of the first
+			 * one: measured on this board, a 960x600 S_FMT on
+			 * /dev/video4 changed the sensor to 960x600 while
+			 * scaler.0 was still programmed for 1920x1080, which
+			 * made the ISP DMA run at a null address
+			 *   "Bug is in CSI module, invalid address: 0x0, id:0x2"
+			 *   (IOMMU master 2 = the ISP on sun60iw2)
+			 * and wedged both capture nodes forever in DQBUF.
+			 * Refuse loudly instead of corrupting a live stream.
+			 */
+			if (set &&
+			    (sd->grp_id == VIN_GRP_ID_SENSOR ||
+			     sd->grp_id == VIN_GRP_ID_MIPI ||
+			     sd->grp_id == VIN_GRP_ID_CSI ||
+			     sd->grp_id == VIN_GRP_ID_TDM_RX ||
+			     sd->grp_id == VIN_GRP_ID_ISP) &&
+			    vin_sd_used_by_other_stream(vinc, sd, sd_ind)) {
+				struct v4l2_subdev_format cur;
+
+				memset(&cur, 0, sizeof(cur));
+				cur.which = V4L2_SUBDEV_FORMAT_ACTIVE;
+				cur.pad = 0;
+				/*
+				 * Only the geometry is compared: the first pass of
+				 * the loop above still carries the *requested* mbus
+				 * code (the sensor turns it into its own RAW code
+				 * and the loop repeats), so comparing codes here
+				 * rejected even a plain "same size" S_FMT.
+				 */
+				if (v4l2_subdev_call(sd, pad, get_fmt, NULL, &cur) == 0 &&
+				    (cur.format.width != sfmt.format.width ||
+				     cur.format.height != sfmt.format.height)) {
+					vin_err("%s is already streaming %ux%u, refusing to change it to %ux%u for video%d: stop that stream (or ask for the same size) first\n",
+						sd->name, cur.format.width, cur.format.height,
+						sfmt.format.width, sfmt.format.height,
+						vinc->id);
+					mutex_unlock(&vinc->vid_cap.vdev.entity.graph_obj.mdev->graph_mutex);
+					media_graph_walk_cleanup(&graph);
+					return -EBUSY;
+				}
+			}
+
 			sfmt.pad = 0;
 			ret = v4l2_subdev_call(sd, pad, set_fmt, NULL, &sfmt);
 			if (ret) {
@@ -1310,6 +1420,18 @@
 	int ret;
 	__maybe_unused struct vin_core *vinc_bind = NULL;
 
+	/*
+	 * Check the shared uplink *before* vin_auto_s_input() powers this node
+	 * up: a rejected S_FMT must not leave the video node half-open, because
+	 * that state (sensor powered for this node, pipeline never bound)
+	 * already degraded the *other* camera's stream on this board.
+	 * See vin_pipeline_try_format() for why the size may not change while
+	 * another capture node is streaming from the same CSI.
+	 */
+	ret = vin_check_shared_uplink(vinc, f->fmt.pix_mp.width, f->fmt.pix_mp.height);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+
 	ret = vin_auto_s_input(vinc);
 	if (ret < 0)
 		return ret;
@@ -1382,7 +1504,7 @@
 	ret = v4l2_subdev_call(vinc->vid_cap.pipe.sd[VIN_IND_SCALER], pad,
 				get_selection, NULL, &sel);
 	if (ret < 0)
-		vin_err("v4l2 sub device scaler get_selection error!\n");
+		vin_warn("v4l2 sub device scaler get_selection error!\n");
 	else
 		s->r = sel.r;
 	return ret;
@@ -2266,6 +2388,37 @@
 	mutex_lock(&cap->vdev.entity.graph_obj.mdev->graph_mutex);
 	vin_timer_init(cap->vinc);
 
+	/*
+	 * The shared uplink (sensor/mipi/csi/isp) is programmed by whichever
+	 * capture node did its S_FMT last, so this node can be left with a
+	 * window that no longer matches what the uplink really produces.
+	 * Streaming in that state is what corrupts the ISP DMA: the scaler
+	 * requests more lines than the ISP sends and the ISP ends up writing
+	 * to a null address ("Bug is in CSI module, invalid address: 0x0,
+	 * id:0x2", IOMMU master 2 = the ISP on sun60iw2), after which both
+	 * capture nodes spin in DQBUF.  Compare what we were configured for
+	 * against what the sensor is actually running and fail early.
+	 */
+	if (vinc->vin_status.width && vinc->vin_status.height &&
+	    cap->pipe.sd[VIN_IND_SENSOR]) {
+		struct sensor_win_size win_cfg;
+
+		memset(&win_cfg, 0, sizeof(win_cfg));
+		if (v4l2_subdev_call(cap->pipe.sd[VIN_IND_SENSOR], core, ioctl,
+				     GET_CURRENT_WIN_CFG, &win_cfg) == 0 &&
+		    win_cfg.width && win_cfg.height &&
+		    (win_cfg.width != vinc->vin_status.width ||
+		     win_cfg.height != vinc->vin_status.height)) {
+			vin_err("video%d was configured for %ux%u but the shared sensor is now running %ux%u (another video node did an S_FMT in between); refusing to start - run VIDIOC_S_FMT on this node again\n",
+				vinc->id, vinc->vin_status.width, vinc->vin_status.height,
+				win_cfg.width, win_cfg.height);
+			vin_timer_del(cap->vinc);
+			mutex_unlock(&cap->vdev.entity.graph_obj.mdev->graph_mutex);
+			vb2_ioctl_streamoff(file, priv, i);
+			return -EBUSY;
+		}
+	}
+
 	if (vinc->dma_merge_mode == 1) {
 		vin_set_next_buf_addr(cap->vinc);
 		if (vinc->id % 4 == 1 && vin_core_gbl[vinc->id - 1]) {
@@ -2350,7 +2503,7 @@
 	int ret = 0;
 	__maybe_unused struct vin_core *vinc_bind = NULL;
 	if (!vin_streaming(cap)) {
-		vin_err("video%d has already stream off\n", vinc->id);
+		vin_warn("video%d has already stream off\n", vinc->id);
 		goto streamoff_error;
 	}
 	vin_timer_del(vinc);
@@ -3869,7 +4022,7 @@
 		clear_bit(VIN_BUSY, &cap->state);
 		clear_bit(VIN_STREAM, &cap->state);
 		if (cap->pipe.sd[VIN_IND_SENSOR])
-			vin_err("%s is not used, video%d cannot be close!\n", cap->pipe.sd[VIN_IND_SENSOR]->name, vinc->id);
+			vin_warn("%s is not used, video%d cannot be close!\n", cap->pipe.sd[VIN_IND_SENSOR]->name, vinc->id);
 		return -1;
 	}
 
@@ -4773,7 +4926,7 @@
 	mutex_lock(&cap->vdev.entity.graph_obj.mdev->graph_mutex);
 	if (!cap->pipe.sd[VIN_IND_SENSOR] || !cap->pipe.sd[VIN_IND_SENSOR]->entity.use_count) {
 		clear_bit(VIN_BUSY, &cap->state);
-		vin_err("%s is not used, video%d cannot be close!\n", cap->pipe.sd[VIN_IND_SENSOR]->name, vinc->id);
+		vin_warn("%s is not used, video%d cannot be close!\n", cap->pipe.sd[VIN_IND_SENSOR]->name, vinc->id);
 		mutex_unlock(&cap->vdev.entity.graph_obj.mdev->graph_mutex);
 		return -1;
 	}
--- a/bsp/drivers/vin/vin-vipp/sunxi_scaler.c
+++ b/bsp/drivers/vin/vin-vipp/sunxi_scaler.c
@@ -265,7 +265,20 @@
 		sel->r = scaler->crop.request;
 		break;
 	default:
-		return -EINVAL;
+		/*
+		 * A target this subdev does not implement is not an error in
+		 * itself: G_SELECTION(COMPOSE) on the sink pad is what every
+		 * generic V4L2 client asks for (v4l2-ctl --all, libisp), and
+		 * answering -EINVAL for it made vin_video.c print
+		 * "v4l2 sub device scaler get_selection error!" at ERR level,
+		 * which makes a perfectly healthy board look broken in dmesg.
+		 * Report the sink rectangle instead.
+		 */
+		sel->r.left = 0;
+		sel->r.top = 0;
+		sel->r.width = format_sink->width;
+		sel->r.height = format_sink->height;
+		break;
 	}
 
 	return 0;
@@ -484,7 +497,12 @@
 		sel->r = scaler->crop.request;
 		break;
 	default:
-		return -EINVAL;
+		/* same as the state-based helper above - see the comment there */
+		sel->r.left = 0;
+		sel->r.top = 0;
+		sel->r.width = format_sink->width;
+		sel->r.height = format_sink->height;
+		break;
 	}
 
 	return 0;
--- a/bsp/drivers/vin/vin-isp/sunxi_isp.c
+++ b/bsp/drivers/vin/vin-isp/sunxi_isp.c
@@ -3374,9 +3374,25 @@
 		} else if (isp->work_mode == 0xff) {
 			logic_isp = glb_isp[isp_virtual_find_logic[isp->id]];
 			if (logic_isp->work_mode == ISP_ONLINE) { /*logic isp work in online*/
-				vin_log(VIN_LOG_VIDEO, "isp%d work in online mode, isp%d cannot to work!\n", logic_isp->id, isp->id);
-				isp->is_empty = 1;
-				goto init;
+				/*
+				 * This device tree node is a *virtual* instance of the
+				 * very same ISP602 that isp%d is already running in
+				 * online mode: it has no register window of its own
+				 * (the reg ranges of isp00/isp01/isp02/isp03 all end at
+				 * 0x5901300) and nothing in the driver can use it.
+				 * Registering it as an empty media entity is not
+				 * harmless: measured on this board (2026-09-16) setting
+				 * `status = "okay"` on isp@58ffffc made the *first*
+				 * camera deliver 0 frames - VIDIOC_S_INPUT returned an
+				 * empty device name, vin0 prs_in.y stayed 0 and the
+				 * kernel logged nothing at all.
+				 * Refuse the probe, so a misconfigured device tree fails
+				 * loudly instead of quietly killing a working stream.
+				 */
+				vin_err("isp%d@%pOF: refusing to register, it is a virtual instance of isp%d which already works in online mode; keep this node disabled in the device tree, enabling it makes the running camera stop delivering frames with no error at all\n",
+					isp->id, np, logic_isp->id);
+				ret = -ENODEV;
+				goto freedev;
 			}
 		}
 	}
